.TH WOLF 1 "September 2026" "WOLF 0.1.0.dev0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wolf \- manage WOLF EDA environments and inspect available workflow support
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wolf
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
WOLF is an EDA workflow and environment manager. The installed command currently
provides legacy-compatible environment management, process discovery, backend
inspection, pinned local package installation, resolved run planning/execution,
and basic installation diagnostics.
.PP
A WOLF environment represents an EDA experiment environment. It is not a Python
virtual environment.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " --help"
Show command help and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Show the installed WOLF version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT COMMANDS
.TP
.B wolf env list
List environments in deterministic name order. An empty collection is not an
error.
.TP
.BI "wolf env create " name
Create a legacy-compatible environment and its current metadata files.
.TP
.BI "wolf env create " "name --from file"
Validate and import a versioned declarative environment. Relative stored paths
resolve from the input file's directory.
.TP
.BI "wolf env clone " "source destination"
Clone a declarative-v1 profile without duplicating installed packages.
.TP
.BI "wolf env remove " name
Remove an environment after interactive confirmation.
.TP
.BI "wolf env remove --yes " name
Remove an environment without prompting.
.TP
.BI "wolf env set " "name key value"
Persist a legacy variable, or update an existing structured path such as
.B constraints.clocks.0.period_ps
in a declarative profile.
.SH PROCESS COMMANDS
.TP
.B wolf process list
List legacy process definitions found in the WOLF state directory.
.SH BACKEND COMMANDS
.TP
.B wolf backend list
List built-in execution backends. The current built-ins are
.B cadence-flowtool
and
.BR orfs .
.TP
.BI "wolf backend info " name
Show a backend's description, compatibility adapter, and backend-local
dependency status.
.SH PACKAGE COMMANDS
.TP
.B wolf package list
List installed packages with pinned revision, status, and absolute location.
.TP
.BI "wolf package info " kind/name
Show built-in registry metadata and local installation status for a package.
.TP
.BI "wolf install " kind/name
Fetch, check out, validate, and install a package at its pinned revision under
the configured package store (the XDG data root by default).
Repeated installation of the same valid revision is idempotent.
.SH CONFIGURATION AND REGISTRIES
.TP
.B wolf config list|path|get|set|unset|edit
Inspect or update the versioned XDG global configuration. Values are validated
and written atomically.
.TP
.B wolf init
Run the line-oriented first-use configuration wizard. Existing configuration
and shell startup files are never overwritten without confirmation.
.TP
.B wolf registry list
List the built-in and configured package-manifest registries.
.TP
.BI "wolf registry add " "name source"
Add a local or Git registry (use --type when inference is not unambiguous).
.TP
.B "wolf registry info|sync|remove NAME"
Inspect, explicitly synchronize, or remove a configured registry.
.SH RUN COMMAND
.TP
.B wolf run --plan
Resolve and display the selected environment's experiment paths without
executing tools. The displayed workspace and run paths are independent of the
caller's current directory. Planning does not allocate a physical run.
.TP
.BI "wolf run --environment " name
Resolve a named legacy or declarative environment and invoke the existing runner.
Stored relative paths resolve from the environment directory. An explicit
.B --workspace
path is relative to the invocation directory unless absolute.
Real declarative executions freeze
.I wolf.resolved.yaml
inside the exact allocated run before invoking backend stages. The historical
manifest is never overwritten by a changed environment.
.TP
.B wolf run --clean
Allocate a new numbered implementation run and freeze provenance into that
exact allocation before backend execution.
.TP
.B wolf run -y
Skip pre-run and per-stage confirmations while retaining progress and result
summaries.
.TP
.BI "wolf activate " name
With the Bash integration loaded, activate the selected WOLF environment in the
current shell. It does not change the working directory. Use
.B wolf deactivate
to restore the original prompt and remove WOLF shell state.
.TP
.BI "wolf info " "[name]"
Show one environment using resolved semantic fields. The name may be omitted
when a WOLF environment is active; otherwise it is required.
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION AND COMPLETION
Source
.I shell/wolf.bash
from Bash or
.I shell/wolf.zsh
from zsh to enable current-shell activation and native command completion.
Completion currently includes public commands, subcommands and options, stored
environment names, built-in backend names, and built-in package identifiers. It will be extended as later
CLI interfaces become stable. Automatic shell startup-file integration is not
yet installed.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B wolf doctor
Report the WOLF and Python versions, operating system, state root, Git
availability, package-store writability, and optional Docker and Podman availability. EDA tools are not
global WOLF requirements and are not checked here.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WOLF_HOME
Compatibility/testing override for WOLF-owned data stores. Persistent global
configuration is found through XDG at
.IR ~/.config/wolf/config.yaml .
.SH EXIT STATUS
WOLF exits with status 0 on success and nonzero on invalid input, missing state,
an unavailable operation, or command failure.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/wolf/environments
Default environment collection (legacy `WOLF_HOME/envs` remains supported).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/wolf/processes
Default process definition collection (legacy `WOLF_HOME/config` remains supported).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/wolf/packages
Default versioned local package store.
.TP
.I ~/.wolf/generated/environments
Deterministic generated backend configuration and resolved planning manifests
for declarative environments.
.SH SEE ALSO
Project architecture, backend, and testing documentation is maintained under
the repository's
.I docs
directory.
